WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.200
-Our first guest tonight is

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.901
a very talented actress
and producer.

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:06.267
Her latest project, "Erased:
The Murder of Elma Sands,"

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:07.901
is available now on
Apple Podcasts.

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:10.133
Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:11.367
Allison Williams, everybody.

00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:13.634
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:29.200
Welcome back.
-Thank you for having me back.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.067
-A lot's changed since
the last time you were --

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:34.567
Arlo -- you have a baby,
who is turning 2 in November.

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:35.968
-Yeah.
-How is it?

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:37.400
-It is so wild.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.534
He's, like, a full --
When I watch baby videos

00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:42.167
of him and now, it feels like
I'm watching him trapped

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:43.601
in a body that didn't, like --

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:45.334
didn't perform in the way
that he needed.

00:00:45.334 --> 00:00:46.701
-Yeah.

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:48.901
-I watch him, like, make the
sounds that newborns make,

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:51.968
and I'm like, "Oh, my God, he
was trying to tell us so much."

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:53.434
And we didn't know.

00:00:53.434 --> 00:00:55.300
We just thought he was, like --
you know, had a dirty diaper

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.100
or something, but he was trying
to ask very complex questions.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.934
-Yes. It is funny how
that you can --

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:02.400
You can't think --
You can look backwards.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.734
You can see him now and --
-Yes. You can't project forward.

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:05.200
-You can't project forward,
but you can go,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:06.868
"Oh, yeah, you were gonna be
this the whole time."

00:01:06.868 --> 00:01:09.467
-Yeah, and he is, like,
just consistently that guy.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.634
-Yeah. And now you're
coming up -- It's Halloween,

00:01:11.634 --> 00:01:12.834
and you're in that sweet spot

00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:14.300
where you still get to
decide the costume.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:18.033
-We get to decide, which meant
a bear costume on the Internet

00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:19.868
that does not in any way
resemble a bear.

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:20.968
-Okay.

00:01:20.968 --> 00:01:22.901
-It is a heavily air-quoted
"bear."

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:24.501
It's really just --
It has, like,

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.934
a white oval on the stomach and
it's sort of like a puke-brown.

00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:29.234
-Uh-huh.
-Happy.

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:31.067
-And was this sort of a
last-minute purchase

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:32.133
that you got such a bad bear?

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:34.467
-You know,
I understand the question

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:38.234
and the spirit
in which it was asked.

00:01:38.234 --> 00:01:42.701
I'm so embarrassed to say
it was very premeditated.

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:45.367
And it arrived quite a bit ago,
and I have stuck with it.

00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:47.334
There's also a
backup firefighter costume,

00:01:47.334 --> 00:01:48.567
because he's very
into firefighting.

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:50.100
-Oh, that's more --
It is, I will say --

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.534
-Well, no, let me amend that.
He hasn't put out any fires yet.

00:01:53.534 --> 00:01:57.200
-"He's very into firefighting"
is a very funny thing to say

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.634
about someone who's not yet 2.

00:01:58.634 --> 00:01:59.868
-Yeah.

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:02.167
-You're like, "How often
is he running into fires?"

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:03.167
-Well, here's the --
Yeah, not --

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:04.167
But here's the thing.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:06.901
He's, like, a busy,
like, working --

00:02:06.901 --> 00:02:08.334
I almost said "workingman."

00:02:08.334 --> 00:02:09.901
He has that bearing.

00:02:09.901 --> 00:02:11.734
He wakes up, like,
already kind of overwhelmed

00:02:11.734 --> 00:02:13.133
by all the things he has to do.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.467
He looks outside, he's like,
"Ugh! We have to leaf-blow."

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:17.868
-Okay.
-And I'm like, "Yeah, it's fall.

00:02:17.868 --> 00:02:19.901
It's sort of, like, your big
season for the leaf blower.

00:02:19.901 --> 00:02:21.734
All summer, we were just
moving the grass around,

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:23.267
but now it's like,
this is the big leagues."

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:25.200
-He's like, "I got to start
setting my alarm earlier.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.667
There's too much --
This day goes by too fast."

00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:28.868
-So fast.

00:02:28.868 --> 00:02:30.601
-Do you guys dress up,
as parents?

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:32.334
-No. That's a hard "no."
-Yeah.

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:34.167
-I forgot that that was,
like, a thing

00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:36.200
until someone asked me
a couple weeks ago.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.467
And I was like, "I can't."

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:39.701
I kind of play, like,
a sophisticated version of

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:40.801
dress-up for a living,
so it's --

00:02:40.801 --> 00:02:42.033
-I agree.

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:43.267
-I --
-I also --

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:44.801
-Thank you for agreeing
it's sophisticated.

00:02:44.801 --> 00:02:48.534
-Yeah. Well, it's mostly that I
share the reason behind it,

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:49.901
and I've never thought
it was sophisticated,

00:02:49.901 --> 00:02:52.033
so I'm jumping on like,
"Yes, yes, yes.

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:53.834
That's what it is."
-Yes, yes, yes. Of course.

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:57.667
-Yeah, when -- Even today,
when adults invite us to a party

00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:59.167
and they're like, "And it's,
you know, wear costumes,"

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:00.934
I'm like,
"Or...we have other plans?"

00:03:00.934 --> 00:03:02.267
-Or -- Yeah.

00:03:02.267 --> 00:03:04.467
I once -- I was Waldo one year,
and that was, like,

00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:05.834
a pretty simple --

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:07.234
-That's a good idea.
-That's a good adult costume.

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:09.634
-Yeah. Because people know
what it is,

00:03:09.634 --> 00:03:11.701
so you don't have to have,
like, a long conversation.

00:03:11.701 --> 00:03:14.067
-Exactly. They can really be,
like, vertical or horizontal.

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:15.701
As long as you have
red and white, like, people --

00:03:15.701 --> 00:03:17.334
or glasses --
people just, like, get it.

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:19.634
-When people come up to me
at a party and they're like,

00:03:19.634 --> 00:03:20.868
"Do you get it?",

00:03:20.868 --> 00:03:23.234
I always want to be like,
"I don't get any of it.

00:03:23.234 --> 00:03:25.100
I don't get what
we're even doing here."

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.300
-What is this holiday?
-Yeah.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.901
I understood dressing up if,
in return for it,

00:03:28.901 --> 00:03:30.167
an old person gave me candy,

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:31.501
but what we're doing now
is beyond me.

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:33.100
-As adults is so true.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.501
-Beyond me.

00:03:34.501 --> 00:03:36.667
-And it's cold,
and everyone wants to be sexy.

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:38.067
The other thing is,
when you see it through

00:03:38.067 --> 00:03:40.400
a kid's point of view,
it's all so arbitrary.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.701
-Yeah.

00:03:41.701 --> 00:03:44.534
-Like, you pass, like, a pumpkin
with a face carved in it,

00:03:44.534 --> 00:03:46.834
and I'm like,
"I don't know where to start."

00:03:46.834 --> 00:03:50.601
We have all kinds of
plants and gourds around us

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:52.467
that we don't hack away at.

00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:53.801
-Yeah.

00:03:53.801 --> 00:03:55.634
-And, yet, this particular thing

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:59.300
we've decided we're just
gonna demolish once a year.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.734
It's so strange.

00:04:00.734 --> 00:04:02.601
It's like -- And, yeah,
why do people put skeletons --

00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:04.367
How do you explain a skeleton?
-I know. That's the thing.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:06.834
My kids are already asking me
enough questions without more.

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:08.000
-Yes.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.267
-Cool it with
your front-steps skeletons.

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:12.601
-Yeah, they're very elaborate
and, sometimes, music's playing

00:04:12.601 --> 00:04:13.868
and they're kind of scary.

00:04:13.868 --> 00:04:15.567
-When my kids are like,
"Why is there a skeleton

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:16.968
on the front steps
of that brownstone,"

00:04:16.968 --> 00:04:19.367
I'm like,
"Everyone in there died."

00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:20.834
-That's a really --

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:23.901
-That's how you know everybody
in that house died and that --

00:04:23.901 --> 00:04:26.300
-I love -- You have such
a gentle touch as a dad.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.667
-Yeah. Well, if you don't say it
like a bad thing,

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:29.834
they're just like, "Okay, cool."

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:31.567
If you're not like,
"Everyone in there died."

00:04:31.567 --> 00:04:32.767
Then they're like, "Aah!"

00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:34.067
But they're like,
"Yeah, everyone in there died

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:35.434
and you're gonna die one day,
too, and it's fine."

00:04:35.434 --> 00:04:37.434
-And so am I.
-We're all gonna die.

00:04:37.434 --> 00:04:39.968
It's crazy for you
to go to school

00:04:39.968 --> 00:04:42.400
thinking you're gonna
live forever.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.534
-Or do anything.
-Or do anything.

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:46.400
-Assuming you're gonna
live forever.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.767
-I want to ask about
your parents, now grandparents.

00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:51.167
-Yes. They are still my parents,

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:52.367
but they have also
become grandparents.

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:53.868
-Yeah. It's really cool
they weren't like --

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:55.133
-They didn't become
my grandparents.

00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:57.067
-They're like,
"We have bad news."

00:04:57.067 --> 00:04:58.501
-"You don't have parents
anymore. But -- wait."

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:00.300
-"But good news --
we're your new grandparents."

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.701
-Yeah. No, they are --

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:04.067
-So, Brian and Jane --
how are they taking to it?

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:05.300
-They're obsessed.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.234
I mean, like,
I don't know how to describe --

00:05:07.234 --> 00:05:08.367
-First-timers, right?
-First-timers.

00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:10.267
I don't know how to describe
the love because

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:12.267
I've never experienced it,
but they are, like...

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:14.067
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:17.367
-It's true.

00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:18.534
-That is not --
-Did you realize?

00:05:18.534 --> 00:05:19.934
-No, I did not realize
what I was saying.

00:05:19.934 --> 00:05:21.667
-Did you realize you,
all of a sudden, were in therapy

00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:23.767
and you forgot
all these people were here?

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:25.868
-Well, I didn't know you had
an audience of just therapists.

00:05:25.868 --> 00:05:28.667
-Oh, yeah.
-Very weird choice.

00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:30.100
-It's Therapist Wednesday.
-Oh, amazing!

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.968
-Yeah.
-Hello.

00:05:32.968 --> 00:05:34.934
I meant that I haven't
experienced the love of

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:38.968
a grandparent, as Arlo is
only 2, as is well-established.

00:05:38.968 --> 00:05:41.200
So -- But they were like,
"Yeah, I assume

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.634
we're gonna be crazy about him."

00:05:42.634 --> 00:05:44.734
And then what's happened is
now I get texts that are like,

00:05:44.734 --> 00:05:45.901
"I need a hit.

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:47.601
I need a fix.

00:05:47.601 --> 00:05:49.300
I'm coming by
just to smell his head"

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.567
Or like, "I want to get him out
of the nap to get the breath."

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:55.234
And I'm like, "Okay,
do whatever you need to do."

00:05:55.234 --> 00:05:57.534
And they're so great with --
I just am very lucky.

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:58.934
They're very close.

00:05:58.934 --> 00:06:01.267
-Have -- What does he call them?
I'm always fascinated by --

00:06:01.267 --> 00:06:03.501
-The verdict ended up --
My dad is Bub.

00:06:03.501 --> 00:06:04.868
-Oh, that's good.
-Bub. Bub.

00:06:04.868 --> 00:06:06.601
-Yep.
-It's, like, short and sweet.

00:06:06.601 --> 00:06:09.701
-Yep.
-And then my mom is "J.J."

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:11.968
But Arlo speaks
French and English,

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:14.234
so it's kind of like, "Gi-Gi."

00:06:14.234 --> 00:06:15.300
-Oh! That's real good.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.033
-And we don't know
how to spell it.

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:20.968
So he's gonna have to,
like, grow up, start spelling,

00:06:20.968 --> 00:06:22.133
and tell us how it's spelled.

00:06:22.133 --> 00:06:24.133
-He's gonna
have to reverse-engineer

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:25.567
his baby name for your mother.

00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:28.667
-Exactly. Also, for any future
grandchildren, like, sorry.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:30.367
This is just
the established name.

00:06:30.367 --> 00:06:32.367
-Right. The first kid
establishes the name --

00:06:32.367 --> 00:06:33.701
-It's a big responsibility.
-Yeah.

00:06:33.701 --> 00:06:36.367
So -- 'Cause we call --
My parents are the "pankas"

00:06:36.367 --> 00:06:40.434
because before my son could say
"grandpa," he said "panka."

00:06:40.434 --> 00:06:42.767
-Aww!
-So now we call them the pankas.

00:06:42.767 --> 00:06:45.534
And it's so funny when you say
it in front of other people,

00:06:45.534 --> 00:06:47.767
'cause they just assume that,
like, my parents are

00:06:47.767 --> 00:06:51.033
like Old World Romanians.

00:06:51.033 --> 00:06:52.434
Like, they just,
like, wrap shawls.

00:06:52.434 --> 00:06:54.701
They're like,
"We are the Pankas."

00:06:54.701 --> 00:06:56.400
I have a bunch of questions
to ask you.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.300
We're gonna be right back
with more from Allison Williams.

